import{_ as a,o as t,c as i,ao as r}from"./chunks/framework.C_BEWuzR.js";const c=JSON.parse('{"title":"Choose R, Julia, or the bridge","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"choose-r-julia-bridge.md","filePath":"choose-r-julia-bridge.md","lastUpdated":null}'),l={name:"choose-r-julia-bridge.md"};function o(s,e,n,h,d,u){return t(),i("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[r('<h1 id="Choose-R,-Julia,-or-the-bridge" tabindex="-1">Choose R, Julia, or the bridge <a class="header-anchor" href="#Choose-R,-Julia,-or-the-bridge" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Choose R, Julia, or the bridge {#Choose-R,-Julia,-or-the-bridge}&quot;">​</a></h1><div class="gllvm-route gllvm-route--start"><div><span class="gllvm-route__eyebrow">Pick a route</span><p>These are companions with partial parity, not interchangeable copies of the same workflow.</p></div></div><p>GLLVModels.jl is the matrix-first Julia companion to R <code>gllvmTMB</code>. The R package remains the formula-first model surface and the richer applied article set. The packages share core estimands; they do not offer identical workflows, and they are not a menu of interchangeable Julia optimisers.</p><h2 id="Use-R-gllvmTMB" tabindex="-1">Use R (<code>gllvmTMB</code>) <a class="header-anchor" href="#Use-R-gllvmTMB" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Use R (`gllvmTMB`) {#Use-R-gllvmTMB}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Start in R when you want the formula-first teaching route or the applied article set.</p><ul><li><p>Get started: <a href="https://itchyshin.github.io/gllvmTMB/articles/gllvmTMB.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">R get-started guide</a></p></li><li><p>What that route currently supports: <a href="https://itchyshin.github.io/gllvmTMB/articles/current-limits.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">current limits</a></p></li><li><p>Spatially structured continuous traits: <a href="https://itchyshin.github.io/gllvmTMB/articles/spatial-models.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">multivariate spatial models</a></p></li><li><p>Repeated multivariate measurements: <a href="https://itchyshin.github.io/gllvmTMB/articles/temporal-ar1.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">temporal covariance</a></p></li><li><p>Repeated survey visits in the experimental integrated-SDM route: <a href="https://itchyshin.github.io/gllvmTMB/articles/integrated-repeated-visits.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">what repeated visits add</a></p></li></ul><p>Those limits belong to the R package. Calling Julia does not lift them.</p><h2 id="Use-Julia-GLLVModels.jl" tabindex="-1">Use Julia (<code>GLLVModels.jl</code>) <a class="header-anchor" href="#Use-Julia-GLLVModels.jl" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Use Julia (`GLLVModels.jl`) {#Use-Julia-GLLVModels.jl}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Start in Julia when you already have a response matrix and want the matrix-first companion. Responses are rows and sites are columns (p × n). The packages overlap only for the documented workflows; check <a href="./gllvmtmb-parity">Capability parity</a> before moving a model between them.</p><h3 id="Move-one-R-matrix-into-Julia" tabindex="-1">Move one R matrix into Julia <a class="header-anchor" href="#Move-one-R-matrix-into-Julia" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Move one R matrix into Julia {#Move-one-R-matrix-into-Julia}&quot;">​</a></h3><ol><li><p>Install Julia 1.10 or later, then install the companion with <code>Pkg.add(url = &quot;https://github.com/itchyshin/GLLVModels.jl&quot;)</code>.</p></li><li><p>If your R response matrix has sites in rows and species in columns (<code>n × p</code>), transpose it before fitting in Julia (<code>Y&#39;</code> gives <code>p × n</code>).</p></li><li><p>Run the <a href="./quickstart">Quick start</a> first fit, then use the conversion table there to translate the model you need.</p></li></ol><ul><li><p>First fit and the R ⟷ Julia conversion table: <a href="./quickstart">Quick start</a></p></li><li><p>Documented R–Julia capability comparison: <a href="./gllvmtmb-parity">Capability parity</a></p></li></ul><h2 id="Use-the-bridge-one-way-R-Julia-only" tabindex="-1">Use the bridge (one-way R → Julia only) <a class="header-anchor" href="#Use-the-bridge-one-way-R-Julia-only" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Use the bridge (one-way R → Julia only) {#Use-the-bridge-one-way-R-Julia-only}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The bridge is <code>gllvmTMB(..., engine = &quot;julia&quot;)</code>. It sends a subset of cross-sectional reduced-rank models from R into Julia through JuliaCall. It is one-way: <strong>R → Julia</strong>. It does not run Julia models back through R, and it does not cover phylogeny, spatial, animal, kernel, or iSDM structure, nor the full <code>traits()</code> formula grammar.</p><p>For the admitted bridge surface versus the wider engine, see <a href="./gllvmtmb-parity">Capability parity</a>. The bridge rejects unsupported structures explicitly rather than silently changing the model.</p><h2 id="What-this-page-does-not-claim" tabindex="-1">What this page does not claim <a class="header-anchor" href="#What-this-page-does-not-claim" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What this page does not claim {#What-this-page-does-not-claim}&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><p>Universal parity, or that every R workflow has an identical Julia counterpart.</p></li><li><p>Calibrated interval coverage on either side.</p></li></ul><p>For planned capability work, see the package <a href="./roadmap">Roadmap</a>.</p>',18)])])}const m=a(l,[["render",o]]);export{c as __pageData,m as default};
